enchilada: BT: fake H4 controller on the com.apple.uart.bluetooth kernel control (btfake=1)

Bluetooth round 2 (PLAN-ENCHILADA.md §8 step 2): let the iOS 16 bluetoothd and BlueTool get past their
hardware checks against a controller that exists only in the kernel, and log every packet they
send, so the next boot shows what a real WCN3990 bridge must answer. Their UART transport is not a
tty: both open a kernel control (PF_SYSTEM/SYSPROTO_CONTROL, SOCK_STREAM), set the line with
getsockopt/setsockopt(TIOCGETA/TIOCSETA) and move plain H4 with write()/read(). bsd/dev/sweet_bt.c
registers that control as "com.apple.uart.bluetooth" (BlueTool's default; bluetoothd needs
BT_HCI_TRANSPORT=H4:POSIX:com.apple.uart.bluetooth in its launchd job, its own default is the
Skywalk variant), keeps the termios per connection, reassembles H4 packets and answers every HCI
command with Command Complete (Command Status for OGF 1): canned return parameters for the
commands bluetoothd's init chain and BlueTool's chip probe read (Read_Local_Version = Broadcom,
HCI/LMP 5.0, subversion 0x420e, which BlueTool maps to BCM4357B1; Read_BD_ADDR = /arm-io/bluetooth
local-mac-address; supported commands, features, buffer sizes, LE sizes and states), status only
for the rest. btfake=1: vendor commands (OGF 0x3f) succeed; btfake=2: they fail with 0x01 as a
WCN3990 would. ACL/SCO/ISO are dropped. /dev/uart.bluetooth, cu.bluetooth, btreset, btwake and
btpoweroff are no-op nodes (bluetoothd access()es the first two, BlueTool access()es btpoweroff
and pulses the others by open/close). Log: "SWEET BT: uN rx <hex>" (host to controller) and
"uN tx <hex>" (controller to host), first 64 bytes of each packet, termios and node open/close
lines, 400 lines per boot, then counts; one registration line at boot and one per disconnect.
Called from bsd_init() after domaininit(). Without btfake (or btfake=0) nothing is registered.

